{"units": [{"id": "eis-word6", "translation": "The preposition ἐς [into] in the poet is said to drop the iota when a consonant follows, but to keep it when a vowel follows. “ἀλλ᾽ ἐς μὲν Μενέλαον [but to Menelaus]"}, {"id": "eis-word7", "translation": "ἐσβάντες κνήμας τ᾽ ἠδὲ λόφον [having gone into the greaves and the crest]"}, {"id": "eis-word8", "translation": "εἰς Ἀγαμέμνονα δῖον [to godlike Agamemnon]"}, {"id": "eis-word9", "translation": "εἰς ἐμὰ δέμνια βάντε [having gone into my bed]"}, {"id": "eis-word10", "translation": "τὼ δ᾽ ἐς δέμνια | βάντε [and the two having gone into the bed]).” This conflicts with ‘οἵδ᾽ αἴψ᾽ εἴσβαινον [these quickly went in]"}, {"id": "eis-word11", "translation": "unless perhaps it was not a prepositional phrase but a compound; ambiguous is “εἰς ὄρος ἢ εἰς κῦμα [to the mountain or to the wave]"}, {"id": "eis-word12", "translation": "for it is also read ἐς κῦμα [to the wave]"}, {"id": "eis-word13", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word14", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word15", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word16", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word17", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word18", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word19", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word20", "translation": "ἐς ἀκοὰν ἐμάν [to my hearing]"}, {"id": "eis-word21", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word22", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word23", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word24", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word25", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word26", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word27", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word28", "translation": "ἐσβέβηκε [has gone in], ἐσθορεῖν [to leap in], ἐσκόμιζε [was bringing in]"}, {"id": "eis-word29", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word30", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word31", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word32", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word33", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word34", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word35", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word36", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word37", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word38", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word39", "translation": "contribution, tax"}, {"id": "eis-word40", "translation": "ἐπεισπίπτειν [to fall upon in addition], ἐπεισφέρειν [to bring in addition]"}, {"id": "eis-word41", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word42", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word43", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word44", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word45", "translation": "ἐς Ἴλιον [to Ilium], ἐς αὔριον [until tomorrow], ἐς ὕστερον [for later], ἐσείδομεν [we saw]"}, {"id": "eis-word46", "translation": "εἰς Ἴλιον [to Ilium], εἰς αὔριον [until tomorrow], εἰς ὕστερον [for later], εἰσείδομεν [we saw]"}, {"id": "eis-word47", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word48", "translation": "in"}, {"id": "eis-word49", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word50", "translation": "in"}, {"id": "eis-word51", "translation": "ἰδέσθαι εἰς [to look upon]"}, {"id": "eis-word52", "translation": "ἰδέσθω δ᾽ εἰς ὕβριν [let him look upon the outrage]"}, {"id": "eis-word53", "translation": "ἰδοῦ — εἰς δὲ θήρειον γραφήν, εἰς δὲ [look — and at the beastly painting, and at]"}, {"id": "eis-word54", "translation": "and"}, {"id": "eis-word55", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word56", "translation": "ἐς τὸ πᾶν [for the whole, altogether]"}, {"id": "eis-word57", "translation": "in every way, altogether"}, {"id": "eis-word58", "translation": "completely, utterly"}, {"id": "eis-word59", "translation": "all, every"}, {"id": "eis-word60", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word61", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eis-word62", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "eisago-orth1", "translation": "to lead in, bring in; to bring a case before judges"}, {"id": "eisago-def1", "translation": "to lead in, bring in"}, {"id": "eisago-def2", "translation": "to bring a case before judges"}, {"id": "eisago-note1", "translation": "which has been accepted for ἄγον ἐς δούλιον αἶσαν [leading into a slavish fate] and can be accepted, as I showed in the Oxford notes by comparing similar examples. However, the metrical form is suspect, as Hermann felt, who proposed πατρῴων τάνδ᾽ ἐσᾶγον αἶσαν [this fate of my fathers I was bringing in]."}, {"id": "eisago-quote1", "translation": "For from my father's house I was bringing in (M has ἐς ἄγον [leading into]) a slavish fate."}, {"id": "eisago-quote2", "translation": "But you, bring in, as you know how, this case to decide."}, {"id": "eisago-quote3", "translation": "I bring the case."}, {"id": "eisago-word1", "translation": "leading into a slavish fate"}, {"id": "eisago-word2", "translation": "I was bringing in this fate of my fathers"}, {"id": "eisaei-orth1", "translation": "forever"}, {"id": "eisaei-def1", "translation": "forever"}, {"id": "eisaei-note1", "translation": "from a short [syllable]"}, {"id": "eisaei-note2", "translation": "from a long [syllable]"}, {"id": "eisaei-quote1", "translation": "But for mortals there will be a great story forever."}, {"id": "eisaei-quote2", "translation": "Having this word, you will praise it forever (M has ἐς αἰεί [forever])."}, {"id": "eisameibo-orth1", "translation": "to enter in exchange"}, {"id": "eisameibo-def1", "translation": "to enter"}, {"id": "eisameibo-quote1", "translation": "who will not allow him to enter in exchange, bearing the image of a most hateful beast's bite."}, {"id": "eisanankazo-orth1", "translation": "to compel"}, {"id": "eisanankazo-def1", "translation": "to compel"}, {"id": "eisanankazo-quote1", "translation": "For, I think, kinship thus compels me."}, {"id": "eisapax-orth1", "translation": "once for all"}, {"id": "eisapax-def1", "translation": "once"}, {"id": "eisapax-quote1", "translation": "For it is better to die once for all than to suffer badly all one's days."}, {"id": "eisaphasma-orth1", "translation": "tearing apart"}, {"id": "eisaphasma-def1", "translation": "tearing apart"}, {"id": "eisaphasma-note1", "translation": "Hesychius: εἰσαφάσματα [tearings apart]: εἰσπτήματα [things that fly in], from the word εἰσ [into] and εἰσαφιέναι [to send into]. Or σπαράγματα [tearings]. Aeschylus in Prometheus Unbound (fr. 199)."}, {"id": "eisaphasma-quote1", "translation": "tearings apart"}, {"id": "eisaphasma-quote2", "translation": "things that fly in"}, {"id": "eisaphasma-quote3", "translation": "from the word 'into' and 'to send into'"}, {"id": "eisaphasma-quote4", "translation": "tearings"}, {"id": "eisaphasma-word1", "translation": "or"}, {"id": "eisaphasma-word2", "translation": "Aeschylus in Prometheus Unbound"}, {"id": "eisbaino-orth1", "translation": "to come upon"}, {"id": "eisbaino-def1", "translation": "to come upon"}, {"id": "eisbaino-quote1", "translation": "This abysmal sea of ruin, not easily passable, has come upon him."}, {"id": "eisballo-orth1", "translation": "to throw in"}, {"id": "eisballo-def1", "translation": "to throw in"}, {"id": "eisballo-quote1", "translation": "Zeus has thrown you into unforeseen misery."}, {"id": "eiseidon_xref1-orth1", "translation": "I saw"}, {"id": "eiseidon_xref1-note1", "translation": "See εἰσοράω [to look upon]."}, {"id": "eiseidon_xref1-word1", "translation": "to look upon"}, {"id": "eiserchomai-orth1", "translation": "to enter"}, {"id": "eiserchomai-def1", "translation": "to enter"}, {"id": "eiserchomai-def2", "translation": "to invade"}, {"id": "eiserchomai-def3", "translation": "to come to mind"}, {"id": "eiserchomai-quote1", "translation": "Do not enter these things any longer."}, {"id": "eiserchomai-quote2", "translation": "How many evil pains enter me."}, {"id": "eiserchomai-quote3", "translation": "Let it never enter you, as I will become womanish."}, {"id": "eiseidon_xref2-orth1", "translation": "I saw"}, {"id": "eiseidon_xref2-note1", "translation": "See εἰσοράω [to look upon]."}, {"id": "eiseidon_xref2-word1", "translation": "to look upon"}, {"id": "eiseko-orth1", "translation": "to come in, to enter"}, {"id": "eiseko-def1", "translation": "to come in, to enter"}, {"id": "eiseko-note1", "translation": "ἐσάξειν [to bring in] Bothius."}, {"id": "eiseko-quote1", "translation": "He seems to be coming in breathing brightly."}, {"id": "eiseko-word1", "translation": "to bring in"}, {"id": "eisthrosko-orth1", "translation": "to leap in"}, {"id": "eisthrosko-def1", "translation": "to leap in"}, {"id": "eisthrosko-quote1", "translation": "before leaping into my house."}, {"id": "eisidein_xref-orth1", "translation": "to see"}, {"id": "eisidein_xref-note1", "translation": "See εἰσοράω [to look upon]."}, {"id": "eisidein_xref-word1", "translation": "to look upon"}, {"id": "eisikneomai-orth1", "translation": "to penetrate"}, {"id": "eisikneomai-def1", "translation": "to penetrate"}, {"id": "eisikneomai-quote1", "translation": "with the winged herdsman's missile penetrating."}, {"id": "eiskomizo-orth1", "translation": "to bring in"}, {"id": "eiskomizo-def1", "translation": "to bring in"}, {"id": "eiskomizo-quote1", "translation": "Bring in this stranger kindly."}, {"id": "eisodos-orth1", "translation": "entrance"}, {"id": "eisodos-def1", "translation": "entrance"}, {"id": "eisodos-quote1", "translation": "And now may they grant me to obtain by far the best of my former entrances."}, {"id": "eisoichneo-orth1", "translation": "to enter"}, {"id": "eisoichneo-def1", "translation": "to enter"}]}